      "text": "Let's denote the length of \\(AE\\) as \\(x\\). Since \\(AE = CF = x\\), we have \\(EB = AF = 1 - x\\).\n\nWhen the square \\(ABCD\\) is folded along the lines \\(DE\\) and \\(DF\\), the points \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) coincide on the diagonal \\(BD\\). This means that the coordinates of \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) before the fold should be symmetric with respect to the line \\(BD\\).\n\nLet's place the square \\(ABCD\\) in the coordinate plane with \\(A\\) at \\((0, 1)\\), \\(B\\) at \\((1, 1)\\), \\(C\\) at \\((1, 0)\\), and \\(D\\) at \\((0, 0)\\). The coordinates of \\(E\\) are \\((x, 1)\\) and the coordinates of \\(F\\) are \\((1, 1 - x)\\).\n\nWhen the square is folded, the points \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) will lie on the diagonal \\(BD\\). Therefore, the coordinates of \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) before the fold are \\((t, 1 - t)\\) and \\((t, t)\\), respectively. Since these points coincide on \\(BD\\), we have \\(t = \\frac{1+t}{2}\\), which simplifies to \\(t = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\nSo, the coordinates of \\(A\\) before the fold are \\(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\). After folding, these points coincide at the intersection of \\(BD\\) and the line through \\(A\\) and \\(C\\), which is the midpoint of \\(BD\\), \\((\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2})\\).\n\nThis confirms our assumption that the fold is along the diagonal \\(BD\\). The line \\(BD\\) has the equation \\(y = x\\). When points \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) are folded onto \\(BD\\), their y-coordinates must be equal, so \\(1 - x = x\\), which gives \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\nThus, the length of \\(AE\\) is \\(\\sqrt{2} - 1\\). Here, \\(k = 2\\) and \\(m = 1\\), so \\(k + m = 2 + 1 = 3\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
