      "text": "In the diagram, we form rectangles, and are led to the equation below (note that $DE=DF$ is given).\n\n\n![image.png](cid:image0_11259db5e5614787b9f42c835f96f7bf.png)\n\nAssume that a rectangle \"square\" with side length $d$ is inscribed in rectangle $EFGH$. By the Pythagorean theorem, the shorter side of rectangle $EFGH$ must have length (note that, by construction, $d^2+1=d^2+2(d+b)$)\n\n\\begin{align*}\nd^2+1&=d^2+2(d+b) \\\\\n1&=2d+b \\\\\nd&=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{b}{2}\n\\end{align*}\n\n(We must be careful here, as there could be two solutions to this system.  This is because we are looking at two rectangles, one in which the origin is in the middle, and the other is in which it's at the end--but it doesn't change the length of side $EF$).\n\nWe now have that one side of $EFGH$ has length $\\left( \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{b}{2} \\right)$. Since there are two of them, their combined area must be $1d$.\n\n\\begin{align*}\n1d &= 2 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{b}{2} \\right) \\\\\n1d &= 1 - b \\\\\nd &= 1 - b\n\\end{align*}\n\nAs a rectangle inscribed in a square, we know that two adjacent sides have the same length, so side $EF$ must have length $1-b$.  By the Pythagorean theorem, we have that\n\n\\begin{align*}\nd^2 &= AE^2 + EF^2 \\\\\n1-b^2 &= \\left( \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{b}{2} \\right)^2 + (1-b)^2 \\\\\n1-b^2 &=  \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{b}{2} + b^2 + 1-2b +b^2 \\\\\n-b^2 &= \\frac{3}{4} + b^2 - b \\\\\n-2b^2 +b - \\frac{3}{4} &= 0 \\\\\n8b^2 - 4b - 3 &= 0\n\\end{align*}\n\nWe can then solve for $b$ using the quadratic formula.\n\n\\begin{align*}\nb&=\\frac{-(-4)\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^2 - 4(8)(-3)}}{2(8) } \\\\\n&=\\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{16+96}}{16} \\\\\n&=\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{112}}{16} \\\\\n&=\\frac{4\\pm4\\) \\sqrt{7}}{16} \\\\\n&=\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{7}}{4}\n\\end{align*}\n\nThis gives us $AE = 1 - b = 1 - (\\pm \\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{7}}{4}) = \\boxed{\\sqrt{7}-1}$. The answer is \\sqrt{7}-1",
